FlightGear 2024.1.7 for AROS - release candidate 1
==================================================

FlightGear is a free flight simulator (https://www.flightgear.org). This is a build of it for AROS,
pc-x86_64 (ABIv1): a release candidate, for people who want to try it and say what breaks:

    https://github.com/terminills/flightgear-aros/issues

Read "What has been tested" at the end before you expect much: so far it has only run in a virtual
machine with software rendering, where it is a slide show.


What you need
-------------

AROS    A pc-x86_64 nightly build that contains commit 21200a1a87 of 2026-11-23, which means the
        nightly of 2026-11-24 or a later one. The programs were built against that commit with
        GCC 15.2.0 and have not been run on anything older.

Display An NVidia or AMD graphics card with AROS's own GL driver for it is what we recommend.
        FlightGear needs OpenGL, and without such a card AROS draws it in software on the processor
        (see "Without a graphics card driver"), which is not usable for flying.
        We have to be plain about this: the recommendation comes from the software figures below,
        not from a test. Nobody has run this build on a hardware GL driver yet. If you do, tell us.

Memory  Tested with 4 GB.

Disk    3.2 GB: 2.8 GB for FlightGear's data, 190 MB for the navigation cache built at the first
        start, the rest for this drawer.

Network For fetching the data once (1.9 GB download). FlightGear itself runs without a network.


Installing
----------

1. Unpack the archive where the 3.2 GB are free. You get the drawer "FlightGear". The .lha and the
   .zip hold the same files; here "lha x" and "UnZip" from AROS's contrib unpacked them.

2. Get FlightGear's data. It is not in this archive: FGUnpack downloads the official data archive of
   FlightGear 2024.1.7 from download.flightgear.org and unpacks it as it arrives into the drawer
   "fgdata" beside the programs (24102 files, 2.78 GB). With the network running, in a Shell:

       CD <where you unpacked>/FlightGear
       FGUnpack

   Here that took 4 minutes. If you already have FlightGear-2024.1.7-data.txz, give its name
   instead and nothing is downloaded (2 minutes here):

       FGUnpack Work:Downloads/FlightGear-2024.1.7-data.txz

3. Start it from the same Shell:

       fgfs

   The first start builds the navigation cache and shows the splash screen while it does. That is
   slow on software rendering (see below). Later starts use the cache.

   If fgfs ends at once with "Required data files not found", step 2 has not been done.

FlightGear keeps its settings, the cache and the scenery it downloads in the drawer ".fgfs" inside
the FlightGear drawer.


Options
-------

fgfs reads the file ".fgfsrc" in its drawer before the command line. It comes with three lines:

    --graphics-preset=minimal-quality
    --disable-sound
    --geometry=800x600

Every option of FlightGear's command line can go there (fgfs --help --verbose lists most of them).
The first line keeps the picture simple; with a graphics card driver you will want to remove it or
pick another preset. The second is explained under "Sound". The third is the size of the window:
the default of 1024x768 does not fit on a 1024x768 screen with the window's borders.


Sound
-----

Sound is switched off in this release candidate, with --disable-sound in ".fgfsrc".

With sound on and the ahi.device of the nightly, FlightGear did not survive in our tests: about
20 seconds after sound started, files it was reading at that moment came out damaged (a texture was
refused with a checksum error although the file was intact), and in 2 of 2 runs that went on the
program stopped with a "Program failed" requester about a minute after sound started. With sound
off the same disk and the same files gave no error.

To try it anyway, take the line --disable-sound out of ".fgfsrc". Sound goes through OpenAL
(Libs/openal.library in this drawer, found there by fgfs) and AHI.


Without a graphics card driver
------------------------------

AROS then draws OpenGL on the processor. It has two drivers for that:

softpipe  is in every nightly and is what you get if you do nothing. Measured in a virtual machine
          (QEMU/KVM, one processor core, the Cessna 172P at Keflavik, simplest graphics preset,
          800x600): a frame every 2 seconds in the cockpit, every 1.5 seconds in the outside
          view. The splash screen stays for 5 minutes on every start and for 13 minutes on the
          first one, which builds the navigation cache.

llvmpipe  is about seven times as fast and is not in the nightly. A copy built from the same AROS
          sources is in this drawer as Drivers/llvmpipe.hidd. To use it until AROS is restarted:

              CD <where you unpacked>/FlightGear
              Execute UseLLVMpipe
              fgfs

          Do this before any other program that uses OpenGL has run, or it may have no effect.
          Measured in the same virtual machine at the same size: 3 frames a second in the cockpit,
          5 to 6 in the outside view; the splash screen is gone after a minute.

Both are a way to see that it works. Neither is a way to fly.


Joystick
--------

FlightGear reads game controllers through AROS's controller subsystem (USB HID). FlightGear's own
joystick files apply, so a stick it knows by name is set up by itself. This has been tested with a
simulated stick only; no real one has been plugged in.


Scenery
-------

The data has the scenery around the default airport (Keflavik, BIKF). For the rest of the world
FlightGear can fetch scenery while you fly: start "fgfs --enable-terrasync" with the network
running. In our test it found the scenery server, fetched the models, the airports and the terrain
around Keflavik (about 1 GB on the disk, into ".fgfs/TerraSync") and showed the cockpit 12 minutes
after the start; most of that time went into unpacking the airports, which happens once.


Known problems
--------------

- Sound: see above.
- Software rendering is too slow to fly: see above.
- One fgfs per start of AROS has been the rule in our tests; a second start in the same session
  has not been tested.
- Only one processor core has been tested.
- Nobody has flown by hand. A script has: it starts the engine and takes the Cessna off the runway
  and up to 1000 ft.


What has been tested
--------------------

All of it in QEMU/KVM with one processor core and 4 GB, on the nightly-equivalent build of AROS
commit 21200a1a87, with the files of this archive:

- Both archives unpacked on AROS with lha and UnZip from AROS's contrib: every file as packed.
- FGUnpack, with the download and from a file: 24102 files each time, checksum of the archive as
  expected.
- A first start and a second start on softpipe, as the drawer comes (sound off, 800x600): cockpit
  and outside view drawn, fgfs left through its own exit.
- A start on llvmpipe after "Execute UseLLVMpipe": the same.
- A take-off flown by a script on llvmpipe: engine start, off the runway, 1000 ft above the ground
  86 seconds after the brakes were released.
- "fgfs --enable-terrasync" on llvmpipe with the network running: scenery fetched as described
  under "Scenery".
- A simulated joystick on llvmpipe: found by name, the controls followed it, also after it was
  taken away and put back.
- With sound on: the failures described under "Sound".

Not tested: real hardware of any kind, a graphics card's GL driver, a real joystick, more than one
processor core, any aircraft but the Cessna 172P, any airport but Keflavik.


What is in the drawer
---------------------

fgfs                 FlightGear 2024.1.7
FGUnpack             fetches and unpacks FlightGear's data
.fgfsrc              options for fgfs
Libs/openal.library  OpenAL (openal-soft 1.19.1 from AROS contrib); the nightly does not have it
Drivers/llvmpipe.hidd, UseLLVMpipe
                     the optional software GL driver and the script that selects it
Licences/            the licences of everything linked into the programs
ReadMe               this text

fgfs and FGUnpack carry their libraries inside (OpenSceneGraph, SimGear, PLIB, curl, OpenSSL, zlib,
FreeType, liblzma and others), so nothing else has to be installed.


